\title{Cross-references, citations & tags}
\import{base-macros}
\tag{feature-demo}
\tag{showcase}
\meta{venue}{Feature gallery}

\p{
  Forester weaves trees together. A core \code{ref} renders a typed reference
  to another tree, showing its taxon: \ref{001c}. A core \code{link} makes an
  explicit hyperlink external, like
  \link{https://www.jonmsterling.com/forester/}{the Forester homepage}, or
  internal to a tree (auto-resolving its route), like \link{002l}{the quiver note}. Inline Markdown links work as well: [the quiver note again](002l).
}

\p{
  Citations use the project's \code{cite} macro, which points at
  \code{\startverb\taxon{Reference}\stopverb} trees and auto-numbers across the page: the HoTT
  foundations \cite{rijke-intro-hott-2022} and a category-theory primer
  \cite{fong-spivak-seven-sketches-2019}.
}

\p{
  Page-level \code{meta} and \code{tag} attach structured metadata this very
  tree carries a \em{venue} metadatum and a \em{showcase} tag (visible in its
  header and queryable via Datalog).
}

\p{
  Finally, \code{transclude} embeds another tree inline. Here is one of the
  cited \code{Reference} trees, pulled in directly the same mechanism
  this whole gallery uses to assemble itself:
}

\transclude{rijke-intro-hott-2022}
